@@s001 Derivatives, gradients and edges
@@s002 dato:f(x) = x²,ora:secant through P and Q,ok:tangent at P
@@s003 f(x) = x² and the point <b>P = (1, 1)</b>. Question: how steep is the curve <b>exactly at P</b>? With one point alone a slope cannot be computed.
@@s004 I take a second point Q, at distance <b>h = 1</b>: Q = (2, 4). The line through P and Q (secant) has slope (4 − 1) / 1 = <b>3</b>.
@@s005 I bring Q closer: <b>h = 0.5</b>, Q = (1.5, 2.25). Slope (2.25 − 1) / 0.5 = <b>2.5</b>.
@@s006 <b>h = 0.1</b>, Q = (1.1, 1.21). Slope <b>2.1</b>. (With h = 0.01 it comes to 2.01.)
@@s007 When h tends to 0 the slopes tend to <b>2</b>: it is the slope of the <b>tangent</b> at P, that is the derivative f′(1). With the power rule: f′(x) = 2x, f′(1) = 2.
@@s008 ok:gradient ∇f,dato:direction u,ora:derivative in that direction
@@s009 dato:signal,ora:window,due:filter,ok:derivative
@@s010 dato:dark pixels (10),ok:bright pixels (50),due:filter weights
@@s011 Derivatives, gradients and edges
@@s012 Lecture 7, second part, and the exercises of week 8. The derivative measures how much a function changes. With several variables it becomes the <b>gradient</b>. On a sampled signal it is computed with a <b>filter</b>: in an image the edges are the points where the gradient is large. Exam question <b>Q6 (b)</b>. DW chapter 13.
@@s013 The derivative
@@s014 From the secant to the tangent, f(x) = x² at x = 1
@@s015 Slopes computed with Python.
@@s016 The definition
@@s017 The variation of the function divided by the variation of x, with a step h ever smaller. It is also written dy/dx.
@@s018 Example 13.1 of the book: a line has the same slope everywhere.
@@s019 That is why derivatives serve to optimise: the minimum of a loss is where the derivative is 0 (week 14).
@@s020 The rules
@@s021 Power (xⁿ)′ = n xⁿ⁻¹, sum, product, quotient and the <b>chain rule</b>: all with worked examples on the page <a href="nozioni-derivate.html">Derivatives: the rules</a>.
@@s022 Partial derivatives and the gradient
@@s023 Several variables: one derivative for each (example 13.3 of DW, verified with sympy)
@@s024 A function of two variables.
@@s025 <b>Partial derivative</b> with respect to x₁: x₂ is treated as a fixed number. sin(x₂) does not contain x₁, so it gives 0; x₁x₂ gives x₂.
@@s026 Now it is x₁ that stays still.
@@s027 The <b>gradient</b>: the vector of all the partial derivatives. One component per variable.
@@s028 What the gradient says: f(x, y) = x² + 3y at the point (1, 2)
@@s029 Slope 2 moving along x, 3 along y.
@@s030 derivative in the direction u: D<sub>u</sub>f = ∇f · u
@@s031 With u a unit vector. Along x, u = (1, 0): D<sub>u</sub>f = 2.
@@s032 maximal when u points like ∇f: D<sub>u</sub>f = ‖∇f‖ = <mark class="ok">√13 ≈ 3.61</mark>
@@s033 The inner product ∇f · u = ‖∇f‖ cos θ is maximum for θ = 0. The gradient <b>points where the function rises fastest</b>, and its length says how fast. Gradient descent goes in the opposite direction (week 14).
@@s034 Directional derivative = ∇f · u
@@s035 The derivative of a signal is a filter
@@s036 From h → 0 to h = 1
@@s037 A sampled signal (pixels, frames) has no values between one sample and the next: the smallest step is 1. These are the <b>finite differences</b>.
@@s038 It is an <a href="w07-filtri.html">inner product</a> with the weights −1 and 1: a filter. With correlation it is written [−1, 1]; with convolution, which flips, [1, −1].
@@s039 The derivative falls exactly on the pixel and not between two pixels. For f(x) = x² at x = 1: (4 − 0) / 2 = 2, the exact value.
@@s040 differentiating <mark class="no">amplifies the noise</mark>
@@s041 Noise changes from one sample to the next: the differences make it stand out. That is why one first smooths (Gaussian) and then differentiates.
@@s042 Smoothing with the Gaussian g and then differentiating is the same as filtering once with the <b>derivative of the Gaussian</b> (convolution is associative). Verified on a test signal, away from the edges.
@@s043 Finite differences as a filter
@@s044 Gradient of an image and edges
@@s045 An image is a function of two variables, I(x, y). Filtering with a derivative filter in x gives the image of the derivatives I<sub>x</sub>; in y, I<sub>y</sub>. At each pixel the gradient is ∇I = (I<sub>x</sub>, I<sub>y</sub>).
@@s046 Filter
@@s047 for x
@@s048 Idea
@@s049 differences
@@s050 the simplest, very sensitive to noise
@@s051 <b>Prewitt</b>
@@s052 central difference averaged over 3 rows
@@s053 <b>Sobel</b>
@@s054 like Prewitt, with the central row weighing double (a bit of Gaussian smoothing)
@@s055 The filter for y is the same rotated (the transpose).
@@s056 Sobel on a 3 × 3 piece: [[10, 10, 10], [10, 10, 50], [10, 50, 50]]
@@s057 Weights of the filter for the pixels (correlation), then the sum. Right column brighter than the left ⇒ positive.
@@s058 Bottom row brighter than the top one.
@@s059 Large magnitude ⇒ there is an edge. The direction goes from dark to bright and is <b>perpendicular</b> to the edge (here the edge is diagonal). Numbers verified with NumPy.
@@s060 The sign with scipy.ndimage.convolve
@@s061 With <code>convolve</code> the filter is flipped, so Sobel x on a passage from dark to bright (towards the right) gives <b>negative</b> values (verified: −4 instead of +4 on a step from 0 to 1). For the magnitude of the gradient nothing changes; for the direction it does.
@@s062 Where it comes back
@@s063 <b>Canny</b> (preprocessing of <a href="w06-assignment1-gaze.html">Assignment 1</a>) finds the edges from the magnitude of the gradient. <b>HOG</b> (week 11) divides the image into cells and in each makes a histogram of the directions of the gradient, weighted by the magnitude: a feature for classifying. The gradient of a <b>loss</b> is what makes neural networks learn (week 14).
@@s064 Q6 (b)
@@s065 "Filtering for derivatives, gradients, and edges. You may include how these operations can be used to construct features such as HOG." The path: derivative as a limit → finite differences → filter [−1, 1] → noise and Gaussian smoothing → derivative of the Gaussian → I<sub>x</sub>, I<sub>y</sub> with Sobel → magnitude and direction → edges → HOG.
@@s066 Sobel x and y on a diagonal edge
